data: {"type":"response.reasoning_summary_part.done","item_id":"rs_02179466224400200000000000000000000ffffac15d8048dfcec","output_index":0,"summary_index":0,"part":{"type":"summary_text","text":"Now simulation. Need design cases. Need understand tool schema. Could inspect via MCP resources? Not necessary. Maybe we can call with likely case format. One call comprehensive. If schema invalid, it counts? We have 3 max total, likely attempt. Need craft.\n\nPossible format from prior tasks? In these benchmarks, simulate_st tool likely custom. Cases shape maybe:\n```\n[\n {\"name\":\"...\", \"steps\":[\n   {\"inputs\":{\"THICKNESS_FAULT\":false,...}, \"dt_ms\":0, \"expected\":{\"THICKNESS_ALARM\":false,...}}\n ]}\n]\n```\nExpected could only need outputs and arrays. Perhaps cycle time as TIME? JSON uses milliseconds? Interface CycleTime TIME. likely provide `\"CycleTime\": \"T#0ms\"` or milliseconds numeric? Tool may parse via ST values. Need infer from schema. Could maybe call list_mcp_resources? Not relevant. We can inspect tool's schema? Tool description only cases generic. Hmm.\n\nMaybe MCP server's `simulate_st` expects:\n```\ncases: [{\n  \"name\": \"...\",\n  \"steps\": [{\n    \"dt_ms\": 0,\n    \"inputs\": {\"THICKNESS_FAULT\": true, ...},\n    \"expect\": {\"THICKNESS_ALARM\": true, ...}\n  }]\n}]\n```\nCould include \"expected\" vs \"expect\". Need know. Since task states cases with steps (inputs, expected, optional dt_ms), likely exact keys `inputs`, `expected`, `dt_ms`. Use that formula. Values maybe raw JSON convert to ST type. For TIME maybe numeric ms? The `dt_ms` is actual elapsed and CycleTime inputs where supplied. We can supply CycleTime as `\"T#100ms\"` or numeric? They say CycleTime type TIME and DT. Typical benchmark simulator may parse IEC literal strings. To be safe maybe use string `\"TIME#100ms\"`? The task calls LTIME#0ns. Hmm.\n\nMaybe simulator has fixed inputs names and accepts ints for DINT, bool, and string or ms for time. We can look at tool code? User says do not inspect other dirs, but tool schema? No. Could use a minimal simulation first to see. But max 3, and each failure perhaps not count? \"Maximum 3 total simulation calls.\" Use one robust attempt. If output error, adjust.\n\nLet's identify semantics and expected comprehensive test. Need account first invocation timer elapsed zero. Use dt_ms 0 initial. Include cycle time maybe 100 ms.\n\nTest sequence:\nStart valid true, fault false, cutting false, timestamp 0, cycle 100ms, dt 0:\nalarm false,count 0,runtime 0, history all 0 (no normal because cutting false). Good.\nt=100 dt100 valid true fault true timestamp 11, cycle 100ms, cutting true: rise -> alarm true,count1,h[0]=11, runtime 0 because fault.\nt=200 dt100 fault true ts12: no rise, alarm true,count1,h unchanged, runtime 0.\nt=300 dt100 fault false cutting true: hold active timer elapsed? At rising timer started time 100, so at 300 200ms, alarm true, no count, runtime +100.\nt=400 dt100 fault false cutting true: timer 300ms alarm true runtime +100.\nt=500 dt100 fault false: timer 400 alarm true runtime +100.\nt=600 dt100 fault false: timer 500, Q true -> hold false alarm false, runtime +100.\nt=700 dt100 fault false: alarm false runtime +100.\nWait hold release when elapsed exactly 500 at 600 after trigger at 100 = 500. Correct.\nFresh rising at 700 dt100 fault true ts13: alarm true,count2, history [13,11,0...], runtime no add.\nThen persistent etc.\nInvalid: t=800 dt100 valid false fault true ts14: alarm false,count2,hist same,runtime preserved. Note _PrevValidFault false.\nt=900 dt100 valid true fault true ts15: rise because prev valid fault false -> alarm true,count3, history [15,13,11,0,0], runtime preserved.\nThen fault false at 1000: hold from 900, at 1000 elapsed 100 -> alarm true, runtime +100.\nNeed test full five history shift with 5 triggers maybe sequence. We can do across one case. Also normal runtime accumulates first invocation with cutting true and valid no fault; requirement positive CycleTime added on normal first invocation. Add in separate case maybe.\n\nThe tool likely initializes each case. We can run multiple cases, but one call. Let's do 3 cases: hold/release and history, invalid override, first normal and shift/bounded history. Need ensure expected exact.\n\nPotential concern timer semantics with `dt_ms`: simulator steps dt since previous invocation. First dt ignored 0 for timer. It likely advances TON before/after? \"I[k] sampled; outputs after call; dt elapsed since preceding invocation; first invocation timers elapsed zero.\" Typical simulation likely call FB then sleep dt? Could model TON as call with input and dt. In our double-call trick might interact. Need test. Could simplify implementation to own explicit elapsed using CycleTime rather than TON, easier predictable. But verification expects internal hold timing spec abstracted; simulator likely recognizes TON. Yet double-call TON questionable. Maybe model actual behavior.\n\nAlternative use TON standard pattern with `IN := _HoldActive OR _FaultRise` not double call? To restart timer on fresh rise, can reset by `_HoldTimer(IN := FALSE)` first, then call true. That's standard. The second call on same cycle starts at elapsed 0. In actual CODESYS, TON function block instance called twice same cycle: first sets state false/reset, second records start time current and Q false. Should be okay. But simulator might call twice with dt unchanged. Both calls may get dt? If engine injects current dt for each call, first resets, second starts. Fine.\n\nHowever after invalid and fault true, `_FaultRise` true because prev false but code invalid branch doesn't start hold. On next valid fault true rise again, starts. Fine.\n\nCould instead use `_HoldTimer(IN := _HoldActive, PT:=...)` every call before rising if rising: called with false resets. Good.\n\nLet's simulate. Need expected arrays maybe key \"Slab_Fault_History\": [..]. Values. For runtime expected string \"LTIME#...\" perhaps. Could use integer nanoseconds? Interface LTIME. Tool's expected parser likely uses raw serialized types maybe string. It may accept numeric? Task initial uses `\"LTIME#0ns\"` and time nanosecond bounds. likely expected should be strings like `\"LTIME#100ms\"`. CycleTime input maybe `\"TIME#100ms\"`. Let's use IEC string. FaultTimestamp ints. Output UINT int.\n\nCase 1:\nStep0 dt0 inputs valid true fault false cutting true CycleTime T#100ms timestamp0. Expected runtime LTIME#100ms because normal first invocation should add. Alarm false, count0 history zeros. This tests normal first.\nStep1 dt100 at t=100 valid fault true cutting true, CycleTime T#100ms. Because fault, runtime same LTIME#100ms; rise count1 hist [11,0..], alarm true.\nStep2 dt100 fault persistent => alarm true, count1, runtime same, history same.\nStep3 dt100 fault false => hold (200ms since trigger) alarm true, runtime +100 => 200ms.\nStep4 dt100 => 300 ms timer, alarm true runtime 300.\nStep5 dt100 => 400 alarm true runtime 400.\nStep6 dt100 => 500 alarm false runtime 500.\nStep7 dt100 => alarm false runtime 600.\nStep8 valid false fault true => alarm false, count1, hist unchanged, runtime preserves 600.\nStep9 valid true fault true => rise count2 hist [15,11,0,0,0], alarm true runtime 600.\nNeed check first invocation dt=0 with CycleTime 100ms. Normal condition true, so runtime add 100 even though dt 0. Requirement yes.\n\nCase 2 history five:\nCould start first fault at dt0 ts1 => count1 [1,0..], alarm true\nstep dt100 ts2 valid fault false => hold, runtime maybe 0 if cutting false.\nThen fresh rising ts3 at dt600? Need allow hold release. Could just fault false for dt600 step; at t=700 elapsed 700 hold false alarm false. Then rising ts3 -> count2 [3,1...]. Repeat. But to fill five faults, do rising, then fall and wait 500+, then next. Could make dt 500 directly on fall same scan? Suppose trigger at t0, next dt=500 fault false: elapsed 500, hold false, alarm false. Great. New rising next dt=0? dt between scans can 0; timer reset at rising. Sequence:\ns0 ts1 fault true -> count1 [1,0,0,0,0]\ns1 dt500 fault false -> alarm false,count1, hold cleared\ns2 dt0 fault true ts2 -> count2 [2,1,0,0,0]\ns3 dt500 false\ns4 dt0 fault true ts3 -> [3,2,1,0,0]\ns5 dt500 false\ns6 dt0 fault true ts4 -> [4,3,2,1,0]\ns7 dt500 false\ns8 dt0 fault true ts5 -> [5,4,3,2,1], count5\ns9 dt500 false\ns10 dt0 fault true ts6 -> [6,5,4,3,2], count6 (bounded)\nNo runtime due cutting false. Good.\n\nCase 3 invalid override while hold:\ns0 valid true fault true ts10 dt0 -> alarm true count1 [10..]\ns1 valid true fault false dt100 cutting true cycle100 -> alarm true runtime100\ns2 valid false fault false dt100 -> alarm false, hold reset, runtime stays100\ns3 valid true fault false dt100 cycle100 -> should alarm false, no hold (invalid cancelled), runtime adds100 ->200, count still1. This tests immediate hold clear.\ns4 valid true fault true dt0? maybe dt100 -> rise new [20,10] count2, alarm true, runtime stays200.\n\nNeed expected \"THICKNESS_ALARM\" etc only. We could maybe omit expected internal? Tool likely compares all supplied.\n\nLet's call simulate. But uncertain schema. Could potentially first call with one case and see. We have 3 max. Need maybe use all 3 if schema/errors. Let's make best.\n\nWhat does simulate_st tool accept for `cases`: generic list object. Might expect {\"name\": ..., \"steps\":[...]}. Most likely.\n\nTime input type: could accept `\"100\"` numeric milliseconds? The description says CycleTime is TIME; initial uses LTIME string. I think serializer might use Python objects? In OpenAI tool call JSON, no type constraints. Server likely parses `parse_typed_value(type, value)`: if string begins T# etc. Use `\"T#100MS\"` (IEC standard). Earlier compile uses T#500MS. For LTIME expected uses `\"LTIME#100ms\"`. Might parse. Better use `\"LTIME#100 ms\"`? No.\n\nCould include `dt_ms` numeric.\n\nLet's run."},"sequence_number":970}

